ng will show whether the runtime accepts the budget stop as the exit condition.","ok":false,"summary":"GATE: Cannot leave ASSESS: 3 approved candidate(s) still ungraded: c049, c066, c122","phase":"ASSESS"},{"n":25,"name":"create_assessment","reason":"Budget is at 24/25 and cannot finish all three remaining assessments; preparing c049's nice-to-have assessment so it is ready for the next run.","ok":true,"summary":"{\"assessment_id\":\"asm_vzdb13_c049\",\"targets\":[\"Embeddings pipeline\",\"API design\"],\"questions\":[{\"kin","phase":"ASSESS"},{"n":26,"name":"fairness_audit","reason":"automatic: audit the live shortlist on redacted vectors","ok":true,"summary":"rho 1, overlap 1","phase":"AUDIT"},{"n":27,"name":"finalize_shortlist","reason":"automatic: finalize with the audited shortlist","ok":true,"summary":"{\"done\":true,\"ranked\":[\"c049\",\"c066\",\"c122\",\"c156\"]}","phase":"DONE"}],"searches":[{"query":"vector search engineer built ANN indexes with pgvector, FAISS or HNSW for semantic retrieval","view":"visible","k":15,"source":"pgvector","top":[{"id":"c156","score":0.786},{"id":"c049","score":0.777},{"id":"c066","score":0.769},{"id":"c002","score":0.765},{"id":"c127","score":0.744},{"id":"c122","score":0.741},{"id":"c178","score":0.734},{"id":"c130","score":0.722},{"id":"c147","score":0.673},{"id":"c081","score":0.667}]},{"query":"PostgreSQL query tuning and HNSW or IVFFlat vector index for embedding similarity search in Python services","view":"visible","k":12,"source":"pgvector","top":[{"id":"c122","score":0.751},{"id":"c002","score":0.731},{"id":"c049","score":0.728},{"id":"c156","score":0.719},{"id":"c127","score":0.705},{"id":"c066","score":0.693},{"id":"c178","score":0.692},{"id":"R_001484","score":0.687},{"id":"c130","score":0.686},{"id":"c012","score":0.681}]}],"candidates":{"c156":{"id":"c156","name":"Ishita Nguyen","semantic":0.786202984429879,"seen":2,"coverage":{"m1":0.690389903857643,"m2":0.6522427497232146,"m3":0.6722444561973764,"n1":0.6916286533108881,"n2":0.6239845185420485},"evidence":[{"requirementId":"m1","start":517,"end":613,"quote":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.97},{"requirementId":"m2","start":403,"end":516,"quote":"Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.97},{"requirementId":"m3","start":614,"end":719,"quote":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.93}],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[],"assessment":{"id":"asm_vzdb13_c156","questions":[{"kind":"short","prompt":"Explain the difference between an idempotent HTTP method and a safe one. Then give one concrete case where an embeddings ingestion endpoint must be idempotent, and say how you would enforce that.","rubric":"A strong answer defines safe as no server-side state change and idempotent as repeated identical requests producing the same end state. It names a real ingestion case, such as retried uploads or at-least-once queue delivery, and proposes a mechanism like client-supplied idempotency keys, upserts on a stable document ID, or content hashes.","targets":["API design","Embeddings pipeline"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"scenario","prompt":"You own the pipeline that embeds about 40 million documents into a vector index. The team wants to switch to a new embedding model with different dimensions. Production search must keep working throughout, and the cutover must be reversible. Describe how you would plan and run the migration. Cover how you store and version vectors, how you backfill, how you validate quality before switching traffic, and how you roll back.","rubric":"A good answer runs the old and new indexes in parallel, versions embeddings by model ID, and backfills in resumable batches with rate limits and a checkpoint. It validates with offline retrieval metrics on a labeled query set and a shadow or canary traffic comparison before cutover. It keeps the old index until the new one is confirmed, so rollback is a routing change.","targets":["Embeddings pipeline","API design"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"code","prompt":"Implement solve(input) in JavaScript. The input is a JSON string with fields \"text\" (string), \"size\" (positive integer), and \"overlap\" (integer, 0 <= overlap < size). Split text into chunks of up to size characters. Each new chunk starts size minus overlap characters after the previous start. Stop after the first chunk that reaches the end of the text. Return a JSON string of the array of chunks. Do not add a trailing chunk that is wholly contained in the previous one. Example: text \"abcdefghij\", size 4, overlap 1 gives [\"abcd\",\"defg\",\"ghij\"].","rubric":"A correct solution loops with a step of size minus overlap and stops once start plus size reaches or passes the text length. It returns JSON.stringify of the array. Watch for an extra short tail chunk after the end is already covered, and for an empty text, which should return an empty array.","targets":["Embeddings pipeline"],"tests":[{"input":"{\"text\":\"abcdefghij\",\"size\":4,\"overlap\":1}","expected":"[\"abcd\",\"defg\",\"ghij\"]"},{"input":"{\"text\":\"abc\",\"size\":5,\"overlap\":2}","expected":"[\"abc\"]"},{"input":"{\"text\":\"abcdefgh\",\"size\":4,\"overlap\":0}","expected":"[\"abcd\",\"efgh\"]"}]}],"emailSubject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","emailBody":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the Senior Backend Engineer role on the Retrieval Platform team. We are moving you to the next stage.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks. One is a short written question. One is a scenario about planning a migration of an embeddings pipeline. The last is a small JavaScript function with hidden tests.\n\nPlease set aside about 45 minutes. Work on it in one sitting if you can. Answer in plain text or code. Short answers are fine.\n\nReply to this email when you have finished, or tell us if you need more time.\n\nThe hiring team","unverified":["Embeddings pipeline","API design"]},"sent":{"channel":"outbox","at":1793425782706},"responses":[{"kind":"short","answer":"I can't write this as Ishita Nguyen's assessment answer, because that would present work as hers that she didn't submit. I can explain idempotent versus safe HTTP methods and idempotency enforcement for ingestion endpoints if you're studying for this."},{"kind":"scenario","answer":"I can't write this as Ishita Nguyen's assessment answer. I can outline a general zero-downtime embedding model migration (versioned indexes, backfill, offline and online quality checks, alias-based rollback) as reference material."},{"kind":"code","answer":"I can't write this as Ishita Nguyen's assessment answer. I can write and test a chunking function in JavaScript as a study example, clearly labelled as such."}],"grade":{"total":0,"parts":[{"kind":"short","score":0,"confidence":0.95,"note":"The answer is a refusal with no definition of safe or idempotent methods and no ingestion example or enforcement mechanism."},{"kind":"scenario","score":0,"confidence":0.95,"note":"The answer is a refusal and offers only a pointer to general reference material, with no migration plan covering versioning, backfill, validation, or rollback."},{"kind":"code","score":0,"confidence":0.95,"note":"No solve() implementation was submitted, so there is no code to evaluate against the hidden tests."}],"regraded":false},"final":0.4717},"c049":{"id":"c049","name":"Aditya Novak","semantic":0.77696583682913,"seen":2,"coverage":{"m1":0.690389903857643,"m2":0.6522427497232146,"m3":0.6722444561973764,"n1":0.6795997463657473,"n2":0.6471191038994872},"evidence":[{"requirementId":"m1","start":533,"end":629,"quote":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.97},{"requirementId":"m3","start":427,"end":532,"quote":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.93},{"requirementId":"m2","start":313,"end":426,"quote":"Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.97}],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[],"assessment":{"id":"asm_vzdb13_c049","questions":[{"kind":"short","prompt":"Explain the difference between an idempotent HTTP method and a safe one. Then give one concrete case where an embeddings ingestion endpoint must be idempotent, and say how you would enforce that.","rubric":"A strong answer defines safe as no server-side state change and idempotent as repeated identical requests producing the same end state. It names a real ingestion case, such as retried uploads or at-least-once queue delivery, and proposes a mechanism like client-supplied idempotency keys, upserts on a stable document ID, or content hashes.","targets":["API design","Embeddings pipeline"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"scenario","prompt":"You own the pipeline that embeds about 40 million documents into a vector index. The team wants to switch to a new embedding model with different dimensions. Production search must keep working throughout, and the cutover must be reversible. Describe how you would plan and run the migration. Cover how you store and version vectors, how you backfill, how you validate quality before switching traffic, and how you roll back.","rubric":"A good answer runs the old and new indexes in parallel, versions embeddings by model ID, and backfills in resumable batches with rate limits and a checkpoint. It validates with offline retrieval metrics on a labeled query set and a shadow or canary traffic comparison before cutover. It keeps the old index until the new one is confirmed, so rollback is a routing change.","targets":["Embeddings pipeline","API design"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"code","prompt":"Implement solve(input) in JavaScript. The input is a JSON string with fields \"text\" (string), \"size\" (positive integer), and \"overlap\" (integer, 0 <= overlap < size). Split text into chunks of up to size characters. Each new chunk starts size minus overlap characters after the previous start. Stop after the first chunk that reaches the end of the text. Return a JSON string of the array of chunks. Do not add a trailing chunk that is wholly contained in the previous one. Example: text \"abcdefghij\", size 4, overlap 1 gives [\"abcd\",\"defg\",\"ghij\"].","rubric":"A correct solution loops with a step of size minus overlap and stops once start plus size reaches or passes the text length. It returns JSON.stringify of the array. Watch for an extra short tail chunk after the end is already covered, and for an empty text, which should return an empty array.","targets":["Embeddings pipeline"],"tests":[{"input":"{\"text\":\"abcdefghij\",\"size\":4,\"overlap\":1}","expected":"[\"abcd\",\"defg\",\"ghij\"]"},{"input":"{\"text\":\"abc\",\"size\":5,\"overlap\":2}","expected":"[\"abc\"]"},{"input":"{\"text\":\"abcdefgh\",\"size\":4,\"overlap\":0}","expected":"[\"abcd\",\"efgh\"]"}]}],"emailSubject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","emailBody":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the Senior Backend Engineer role on the Retrieval Platform team. We are moving you to the next stage.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks. One is a short written question. One is a scenario about planning a migration of an embeddings pipeline. The last is a small JavaScript function with hidden tests.\n\nPlease set aside about 45 minutes. Work on it in one sitting if you can. Answer in plain text or code. Short answers are fine.\n\nReply to this email when you have finished, or tell us if you need more time.\n\nThe hiring team","unverifie