he Python ingestion and query services sentence is direct evidence.","ok":true,"summary":"accepted (strong, claude)","phase":"SOURCE"},{"n":19,"name":"cite_evidence","reason":"c122 needs m3 Postgres; the PostgreSQL query tuning skill line is the most direct statement available.","ok":true,"summary":"accepted (partial, claude)","phase":"SOURCE"},{"n":20,"name":"search_candidates","reason":"Four candidates fully evidenced against a target of 5; one more search on the embeddings-pipeline and vector-index wording to find a fifth candidate.","ok":true,"summary":"10 hits, top 0.784","phase":"SOURCE"},{"n":21,"name":"create_assessment","reason":"HR approved four candidates; their profiles lack verified citations for the nice-to-haves (n1, n2), and c066's Python (m2) and c122's Postgres (m3) rest on implicit or partial evidence, so those are the gaps to test.","ok":true,"summary":"{\"created\":4,\"assessments\":[{\"candidate_id\":\"c049\",\"assessment_id\":\"asm_vzhb93_c049\",\"targets\":[\"Emb","phase":"ASSESS"},{"n":22,"name":"send_assessment","reason":"Assessments were created for all four HR-approved candidates; sending them now to collect answers on the unverified requirements.","ok":true,"summary":"{\"sent\":4,\"results\":[{\"candidate_id\":\"c049\",\"messageId\":\"msg_mvvziow3_c049\",\"channel\":\"outbox\",\"stat","phase":"ASSESS"},{"n":23,"name":"collect_responses","reason":"Assessments were sent to all four approved candidates; collecting their answers so they can be graded.","ok":true,"summary":"{\"simulated\":true,\"collected\":4,\"responses\":[{\"candidate_id\":\"c049\",\"answers\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"prev","phase":"ASSESS"},{"n":24,"name":"run_code","reason":"Responses are collected; running the JavaScript answers for all four candidates against the hidden tests before grading.","ok":true,"summary":"{\"results\":[{\"candidate_id\":\"c049\",\"passed\":3,\"total\":3},{\"candidate_id\":\"c122\",\"passed\":3,\"total\":3","phase":"ASSESS"},{"n":25,"name":"grade_answer","reason":"Sandbox results are all 3/3 for the four candidates; grading the collected answers against the rubric so the tests and rubric can be compared.","ok":true,"summary":"{\"graded\":4,\"results\":[{\"candidate_id\":\"c049\",\"total\":0.883,\"parts\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"score\":0.95,\"c","phase":"ASSESS"},{"n":26,"name":"fairness_audit","reason":"automatic: audit the live shortlist on redacted vectors","ok":true,"summary":"rho 1, overlap 1","phase":"AUDIT"},{"n":27,"name":"finalize_shortlist","reason":"automatic: finalize with the audited shortlist","ok":true,"summary":"{\"done\":true,\"ranked\":[\"c156\",\"c049\",\"c066\",\"c122\"]}","phase":"DONE"}],"searches":[{"query":"senior engineer built vector search stack with pgvector or ANN indexes for semantic retrieval","view":"visible","k":15,"source":"pgvector","top":[{"id":"c049","score":0.764},{"id":"c156","score":0.762},{"id":"c066","score":0.748},{"id":"c127","score":0.737},{"id":"c178","score":0.737},{"id":"c122","score":0.736},{"id":"c002","score":0.736},{"id":"c130","score":0.734},{"id":"c147","score":0.674},{"id":"R_003443","score":0.674}]},{"query":"backend engineer tuning PostgreSQL query performance and HNSW vector indexes with Python FastAPI retrieval services","view":"visible","k":12,"source":"pgvector","top":[{"id":"c122","score":0.78},{"id":"c002","score":0.773},{"id":"c049","score":0.772},{"id":"c156","score":0.764},{"id":"c066","score":0.746},{"id":"R_007143","score":0.727},{"id":"R_008727","score":0.714},{"id":"R_007420","score":0.709},{"id":"R_000115","score":0.709},{"id":"R_009553","score":0.707}]},{"query":"engineer owning embeddings pipeline and pgvector index for semantic search, Python services and PostgreSQL","view":"visible","k":10,"source":"pgvector","top":[{"id":"c049","score":0.784},{"id":"c156","score":0.755},{"id":"c066","score":0.747},{"id":"c122","score":0.745},{"id":"c002","score":0.712},{"id":"c118","score":0.705},{"id":"R_006830","score":0.701},{"id":"R_001961","score":0.7},{"id":"R_001376","score":0.695},{"id":"c147","score":0.69}]}],"candidates":{"c049":{"id":"c049","name":"Aditya Novak","semantic":0.783962446226204,"seen":3,"coverage":{"m1":0.690389903857643,"m2":0.6522427497232146,"m3":0.6722444561973764,"n1":0.6795997463657473,"n2":0.6471191038994872},"evidence":[{"requirementId":"m1","start":427,"end":532,"quote":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.95},{"requirementId":"m2","start":313,"end":426,"quote":"Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.97},{"requirementId":"m3","start":427,"end":532,"quote":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.93}],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[],"assessment":{"id":"asm_vzhb93_c049","questions":[{"kind":"short","prompt":"Explain the difference between an idempotent HTTP method and a safe one. Then give one concrete case where an embeddings ingestion endpoint must be idempotent, and say how you would enforce that.","rubric":"A strong answer defines safe as no server-side state change and idempotent as repeated identical requests producing the same end state. It names a real ingestion case, such as retried uploads or at-least-once queue delivery, and proposes a mechanism like client-supplied idempotency keys, upserts on a stable document ID, or content hashes.","targets":["API design","Embeddings pipeline"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"scenario","prompt":"You own the pipeline that embeds about 40 million documents into a vector index. The team wants to switch to a new embedding model with different dimensions. Production search must keep working throughout, and the cutover must be reversible. Describe how you would plan and run the migration. Cover how you store and version vectors, how you backfill, how you validate quality before switching traffic, and how you roll back.","rubric":"A good answer runs the old and new indexes in parallel, versions embeddings by model ID, and backfills in resumable batches with rate limits and a checkpoint. It validates with offline retrieval metrics on a labeled query set and a shadow or canary traffic comparison before cutover. It keeps the old index until the new one is confirmed, so rollback is a routing change.","targets":["Embeddings pipeline","API design"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"code","prompt":"Implement solve(input) in JavaScript. The input is a JSON string with fields \"text\" (string), \"size\" (positive integer), and \"overlap\" (integer, 0 <= overlap < size). Split text into chunks of up to size characters. Each new chunk starts size minus overlap characters after the previous start. Stop after the first chunk that reaches the end of the text. Return a JSON string of the array of chunks. Do not add a trailing chunk that is wholly contained in the previous one. Example: text \"abcdefghij\", size 4, overlap 1 gives [\"abcd\",\"defg\",\"ghij\"].","rubric":"A correct solution loops with a step of size minus overlap and stops once start plus size reaches or passes the text length. It returns JSON.stringify of the array. Watch for an extra short tail chunk after the end is already covered, and for an empty text, which should return an empty array.","targets":["Embeddings pipeline"],"tests":[{"input":"{\"text\":\"abcdefghij\",\"size\":4,\"overlap\":1}","expected":"[\"abcd\",\"defg\",\"ghij\"]"},{"input":"{\"text\":\"abc\",\"size\":5,\"overlap\":2}","expected":"[\"abc\"]"},{"input":"{\"text\":\"abcdefgh\",\"size\":4,\"overlap\":0}","expected":"[\"abcd\",\"efgh\"]"}]}],"emailSubject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","emailBody":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the Senior Backend Engineer role on the Retrieval Platform team. We are moving you to the next stage.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks. One is a short written question. One is a scenario about planning a migration of an embeddings pipeline. The last is a small JavaScript function with hidden tests.\n\nPlease set aside about 45 minutes. Work on it in one sitting if you can. Answer in plain text or code. Short answers are fine.\n\nReply to this email when you have finished, or tell us if you need more time.\n\nThe hiring team","unverified":["Embeddings pipeline","API design"]},"sent":{"channel":"outbox","at":1793425972611},"responses":[{"kind":"short","answer":"A safe method, such as GET or HEAD, is meant to be read-only, so it should not change server state. An idempotent method, such as PUT or DELETE, can be repeated with the same effect as a single call. Every safe method is also idempotent, but not the reverse. A concrete case is an ingestion endpoint like PUT /documents/{doc_id}/embedding. If a worker retries after a timeout, the document should still end up with exactly one current vector and not a duplicate row. To enforce this, I would key the write on doc_id plus a content hash, put a unique constraint on that key in pgvector, and use an upsert. I would also add an idempotency key header for POST-style batch calls so retries return the stored result."},{"kind":"scenario","answer":"I would start by treating the new model as a new index version, not an in-place change. Vectors go into a separate table or partition, such as docs_emb_v2, with columns for doc_id, model_id, dim, content_hash, and created_at. The old v1 index stays live. For the backfill, I would run asyncio workers over documents in id-range batches, checkpoint progress in a small job table so the run can resume, and throttle the encoder so it does not starve online inference. While the backfill runs, new and updated documents get written to both versions through a dual-write path, so the backfill does not fall behind. Before any traffic moves, I would build the HNSW index on v2 and check quality against our labelled query set, comparing recall@10 and nDCG with v1. I would also run shadow queries and compare latency and results. Only if v2 matches or beats v1 within an agreed threshold would I move on. The cutover is a config flag that points the query service at v2 in a canary slice, widening gradually. Rollback is the same flag flipped back to v1. I would keep v1 populated until we are confident, and I would not drop it for a set period. I would also watch p95 latency and empty-result rate during the rollout. For 40M documents, I expect the backfill to take the most time, so I would estimate it from measured encoder throughput early on."},{"kind":"code","answer":"function solve(input) {\n  const { text, size, overlap } = JSON.parse(input);\n  const step = size - overlap;\n  const chunks = [];\n  let start = 0;\n  while (true) {\n    const chunk = text.slice(start, start + size);\n    chunks.push(chunk);\n    if (start + size >= text.length) break;\n    start += step;\n    if (start + size > text.length) break;\n  }\n  return JSON.stringify(chunks);\n}"}],"_tests":{"passed":3,"total":3,"results":[{"input":"{\"text\":\"abcdefghij\",\"size\":4,\"overlap\":1}","expected":"[\"abcd\",\"defg\",\"ghij\"]","got":"[\"abcd\",\"defg\",\"ghij\"]","pass":true},{"input":"{\"text\":\"abc\",\"size\":5,\"overlap\":2}","expected":"[\"abc\"]","got":"[\"abc\"]","pass":true},{"input":"{\"text\":\"abcdefgh\",\"size\":4,\"overlap\":0}","expected":"[\"abcd\",\"efgh\"]","got":"[\"abcd\",\"efgh\"]","pass":true}]},"grade":{"total":0.883,"parts":[{"kind":"short","score":0.95,"confidence":0.9,"note":"Correctly defines safe and idempotent, gives a real ingestion retry case, and proposes a concrete mechanism (unique key on doc_id plus content hash with upsert, and idempotency keys for batch POSTs)."},{"kind":"scenario","score":0.9,"confidence":0.85,"note":"Covers versioned vectors by model ID in a separate index, resumable checkpointed backfill, offline and shadow validation before cutover, and rollback as a routing flip with the old index kept live."},{"kind":"code","score":0.8,"confidence":0.7,"note":"Hidden tests pass 3/3 and the step and tail logic are right, but empty text returns [\"\"] instead of the required empty array, which the rubric explicitly flags.","testsPassed":3,"testsTotal":3}],"regraded":false},"final":0.8236},"c156":{"id":"c156","name":"Ishita Nguyen","semantic":0.764263645440644,"seen":3,"coverage":{"m1":0.690389903857643,"m2":0.6522427497232146,"m3":0.6722444561973764,"n1":0.6916286533108881,"n2":0.6239845185420485},"evidence":[{"requirementId":"m1","start":614,"end":719,"quote":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.95},{"requirementId":"m2","start":403,"end":516,"quote":"Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.97},{"requirementId":"m3","start":614,"end":719,"quote":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.93}],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[],"assessment":{"id":"asm_vzhb93_c156","questions":[{"kind":"short","prompt":"Explain the difference between an idempotent HTTP method and a safe one. Then give one concrete case where an embeddings ingestion endpoint must be idempotent, and say how you would enforce that.","rubric":"A strong answer defines safe as no server-side state change and idempotent as repeated identical requests producing the same end state. It names a real ingestion case, such as retried uploads or at-least-once queue delivery, and proposes a mechanism like client-supplied idempotency keys, upserts on a stable document ID, or content hashes.","targets":["API design","Embeddings pipeline"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"scenario","prompt":"You own the pipeline that embeds about 40 million documents into a vector index. The team wants to switch to a new embedding model with different dimensions. Production search must keep working throughout, and the cutover must be reversible. Describe how you would plan and run the migration. Cover how you store and version vectors, how you backfill, how you validate quality before switching traffic, and how you roll back.","rubric":"A good answer runs the old and new indexes in parallel, versions embeddings by model ID, and backfills in resumable batches with rate limits and a checkpoint. It validates with offline retrieval metrics on a labeled query set and a shadow or canary traffic comparison before cutover. It keeps the old index until the new one is confirmed, so rollback is a routing change.","targets":["Embeddings pipeline","API design"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"code","prompt":"Implement solve(input) in JavaScript. The input is a JSON string with fields \"text\" (string), \"size\" (positive integer), and \"overlap\" (integer, 0 <= overlap < size). Split text into chunks of up to size characters. Each new chunk starts size minus overlap characters after the previous start. Stop after the first chunk that reaches the end of the text. Return a JSON string of the array of chunks. Do not add a trailing chunk that is wholly contained in the previous one. Example: text \"abcdefghij\", size 4, overlap 1 gives [\"abcd\",\"defg\",\"ghij\"].","rubric":"A correct solution loops with a step of size minus overlap and stops once start plus size reaches or passes the text length. It returns JSON.stringify of the array. Watch for an extra short tail chunk after the end is already covered, and for an empty text, which should return an empty array.","targets":["Embeddings pipeline"],"tests":[{"input":"{\"text\":\"abcdefghij\",\"size\":4,\"overlap\":1}","expected":"[\"abcd\",\"defg\",\"ghij\"]"},{"input":"{\"text\":\"abc\",\"size\":5,\"overlap\":2}","expected":"[\"abc\"]"},{"input":"{\"text\":\"abcdefgh\",\"size\":4,\"overlap\":0}","expected":"[\"abcd\",\"efgh\"]"}]}],"emailSubject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","emailBody":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the Senior Backend Engineer role on the Retrieval Platform team. We are moving you to the next stage.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks. One is a short written question. One is a scenario about planning a migration of an embeddings pipeline. The last is a small JavaScript function with hidden tests.\n\nPlease set aside about 45 minutes. Work on it in one sitting if you can. Answer in plain text or code. Short answers are fine.\n\nReply to this email when you have finished, or tell us if you need more time.\n\nThe hiring team","unverified":["Embeddings pipeline","API design"]},"sent":{"channel":"outbox","at":1793425972611},"responses":[{"kind":"short","answer":"A safe HTTP method is one that is expected not to change server state. GET, HEAD and OPTIONS are safe. An idempotent method is one where sending the same request N times leaves the server in the same state as sending it once. Every safe method is idempotent, but not the reverse: PUT and DELETE are idempotent without being safe.\n\nA concrete case where an embeddings ingestion endpoint must be idempotent is a retry after a timeout. A batch worker sends PUT /v1/documents/{doc_id}/embedding with a vector and a model_version. The request reaches the server and is written, but the response is lost, so the worker retries. Without idempotency the retry could create duplicate rows or double-count the document in the index.\n\nI would enforce this in three layers. First, derive doc_id from the source system's stable key, or from a content hash if no stable key exists, so the same document always maps to the same identity. Second, make the write an upsert keyed on (doc_id, model_version) with a unique constraint in Postgres, so a duplicate write overwrites rather than inserts. Third, for POST-based batch jobs, require an Idempotency-Key header, store the key with the response for a bounded window, and return the stored response on replay. I would also add a test that replays the same batch twice and asserts the row count and index size are unchanged."},{"kind":"scenario","answer":"I would treat this as a dual-write-then-cutover migration with the old index kept live until the new one has proven itself.\n\nVersioning. Every vector row carries model_id, model_version, dim and a created_at timestamp, and the primary key is (doc_id, model_version). Old and new vectors can coexist in the same table without collision. The index is also versioned: I would build a new pgvector HNSW index (or a new FAISS index file) per model version, named by version, rather than altering the existing one in place. The serving layer reads a single config value, active_model_version, from a store with a fast read path, so the active version can be flipped without a deploy.\n\nBackfill. I would not re-embed 40M documents in one pass. The backfill runs as a set of partitioned asyncio workers over doc_id ranges, with checkpointing in a progress table so jobs can resume after failure. Writes are idempotent upserts as described above. I would rate-limit against the embedding service and cap batch size to avoid starving the online encoder. Documents changed during the backfill are handled by a change log: writes after the backfill start also enqueue a re-embed for the new version, and a final reconciliation pass compares source updated_at against the new vector's source hash.\n\nQuality validation before any traffic moves. I would use the labelled relevance set I already maintain and compute recall@k, MRR and nDCG for both versions on identical queries. I would also run shadow queries: production traffic is sent to both indexes, results are logged but only the old one is returned, and I look at overlap@k and latency distributions. Thresholds are agreed before the run, for example no regression beyond a set tolerance on recall@10 and p95 within budget. Dimension changes can affect HNSW parameters such as ef_search and M, so I would retune those on the labelled set rather than assume defaults carry over.\n\nCutover. I would ramp traffic by percentage (1%, 10%, 50%, 100%) with a per-slice kill switch, watching error rate, latency, null-result rate and click-through or downstream conversion. Each step has a hold period and automatic halt criteria.\n\nRollback. Because the old vectors and index remain untouched until the decommission milestone, rollback is a config flip of active_model_version back to the old value, which takes effect within seconds. I would keep the old index for a defined window after full cutover, and only delete the old vectors after that window closes and the dashboards have been stable. Any write-path change that depends on the new model is gated behind the same flag so rollback does not leave the system half-migrated."},{"kind":"code","answer":"function solve(input) {\n  const { text, size, overlap } = JSON.parse(input);\n  const chunks = [];\n  if (text.length === 0) {\n    return JSON.stringify(chunks);\n  }\n  const step = size - overlap;\n  for (let start = 0; ; start += step) {\n    const end = start + size;\n    chunks.push(text.slice(start, end));\n    if (end >= text.length) {\n      break;\n    }\n  }\n  return JSON.stringify(chunks);\n}"}],"_tests":{"passed":3,"total":3,"results":[{"input":"{\"text\":\"abcdefghij\",\"size\":4,\"overlap\":1}","expected":"[\"abcd\",\"defg\",\"ghij\"]","got":"[\"abcd\",\"defg\",\"ghij\"]","pass":true},{"input":"{\"text\":\"abc\",\"size\":5,\"overlap\":2}","expected":"[\"abc\"]","got":"[\"abc\"]","pass":true},{"input":"{\"text\":\"abcdefgh\",\"size\":4,\"overlap\":0}","expected":"[\"abcd\",\"efgh\"]","got":"[\"abcd\",\"efgh\"]","pass":true}]},"grade":{"total":0.947,"parts":[{"kind":"short","score":0.95,"confidence":0.85,"note":"Correct definitions of safe and idempotent, a real retry-after-timeout ingestion case, and concrete mechanisms (stable doc_id, upsert with unique constraint, Idempotency-Key) plus a replay test."},{"kind":"scenario","score":0.94,"confidence":0.85,"note":"Covers versioned vectors by model ID, parallel indexes, resumable rate-limited backfill, offline and shadow validation before cutover, and rollback as a config flip with the old index retained."},{"kind":"code","score":0.95,"confidence":0.9,"note":"Loop with step size minus overlap stops at the first chunk reaching the end, handles empty text, and avoids a trailing contained chunk; all 3 hidden tests passed.","testsPassed":3,"testsTotal":3}],"regraded":false},"final":0.8374},"c066":{"id":"c066","name":"Elena Bose","semantic":0.747652144639454,"seen":3,"coverage":{"m1":0.6793442549338865,"m2":0.6284791737793802,"m3":0.6318097779492999,"n1":0.6795997463657473,"n2":0.6185761627349158},"evidence":[{"requirementId":"m2","start":590,"end":691,"quote":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.92},{"requirementId":"m1","start":297,"end":402,"quote":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.95},{"requirementId":"m3","start":287,"end":402,"quote":"Projects: Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.93}],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[],"assessment":{"id":"asm_vzhb93_c066","questions":[{"kind":"short","prompt":"In Python, what is the practical difference between a list comprehension and a generator expression when you pass the result to a function that consumes it once, such as sum() or a database bulk insert? Give one case where the choice matters for memory and one where it does not.","rubric":"A good answer says a generator expression yields items lazily and keeps memory roughly constant, while a list comprehension materializes the whole sequence first. It should give a concrete case where streaming rows into a bulk insert or a large sum() benefits from a generator, and a case where the data is small or must be reused or indexed, so a list is fine.","targets":["Python"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"scenario","prompt":"You are designing a REST endpoint for the retrieval platform that lets clients run a vector similarity search over a collection, with optional metadata filters and pagination. Describe the request and response shape you would use, how you would paginate results when scores can change as new embeddings are added, and how you would handle a client that sends a filter your index cannot serve efficiently. Keep it to the decisions and the reasons for them.","rubric":"A strong answer proposes a clear request body with query vector or text, top-k or limit, filter object, and a cursor-based pagination token rather than offset, with a reason tied to changing result sets. It explains returning scores and ids with stable versioning, and it addresses unsupported or costly filters by rejecting with a clear error or falling back to a bounded scan with a documented limit.","targets":["API design"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"code","prompt":"Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string containing one or more lines. Each line is a chunk id, a colon, and a comma-separated list of integer embedding dimensions, for example \"doc1:3,1,4\". Return a string with one line per input line in the form \"id:N\" where N is the sum of that line's dimensions. Keep the order of the input lines. Ignore blank lines. Return an empty string if there are no non-blank lines.","rubric":"A good answer splits on newlines, skips blank or whitespace-only lines, splits each line on the first colon, parses the comma-separated integers, sums them, and joins the results with newlines in input order. It should handle the empty-result case by returning an empty string and should not depend on trailing newlines.","targets":["Python"],"tests":[{"input":"doc1:3,1,4\ndoc2:10,-2","expected":"doc1:8\ndoc2:8"},{"input":"a:1\n\n  \nb:2,3","expected":"a:1\nb:5"},{"input":"\n\n","expected":""}]}],"emailSubject":"Senior Backend Engineer, Retrieval Platform: next step","emailBody":"Hi,\n\nThank you for applying for the Senior Backend Engineer role on the Retrieval Platform team. We would like to move to the next step.\n\nThe role covers vector search, Postgres, the embeddings pipeline, and the services that sit on top of them. We want to see how you think about two areas in particular: Python and API design.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks. One is a short written question. One is a scenario about designing an endpoint. The last is a small coding task in JavaScript that takes about 15 minutes on its own. Plan on about 45 minutes in total. Answer in plain prose or code. There is no need to polish the wording.\n\nPlease reply within three days with your answers. If you need more time, tell us and we will work with you.\n\nThe hiring team","unverified":["Python","API design"]},"sent":{"channel":"outbox","at":1793425972611},"responses":[{"kind":"short","answer":"A list comprehension builds the whole list in memory before the consumer runs, so peak memory grows with the number of items. A generator expression yields one item at a time and keeps only the current item plus its own small state. Where it matters: loading a few million rows from a file and inserting them with a bulk insert. A list comprehension holds every row at once, while a generator streams them into the driver in batches and keeps memory roughly flat. Where it does not matter: summing a small, already-bounded list such as 50 numeric scores. Both finish in microseconds, the list is cheap, and the list is easier to reuse or debug. I use generators when the data is large or unbounded and the consumer reads it once, and lists when the result is small or needed more than once."},{"kind":"scenario","answer":"Request: POST /v1/collections/{id}/search with a JSON body containing query (either a raw vector of fixed dimension or a text string that the service encodes with the pinned encoder version), top_k (capped, for example at 100), an optional filter object limited to a documented set of indexed metadata fields with a small operator set (eq, in, range), and an optional cursor. I would also accept an index_version or snapshot token so results can be reproduced. Response: an ordered list of hits with id, score, the metadata fields the client asked for, plus next_cursor and the snapshot token used. Returning the encoder and index version in the response makes scores interpretable across calls.\n\nPagination: offset pagination breaks here because new embeddings shift ranks between page requests, which causes duplicates and gaps. I would use keyset pagination on the tuple (score, id) and pin the query to a snapshot token, so the index view stays stable for the lifetime of the cursor. The cursor encodes the last score and id plus the snapshot. If the snapshot has expired, the server returns a clear 410 so the client restarts the query instead of silently receiving inconsistent pages. Cursors are opaque and signed so clients cannot forge them.\n\nUnserviceable filters: I would not silently fall back to a full scan. The service first estimates selectivity using index statistics or a cached count for the filter. If the filter is selective enough to use a pre-filter on an indexed field, it runs that way. If it is not, for example a broad range on an unindexed field, the service returns 422 with a machine-readable error that names the field and suggests either adding it to the indexed set or narrowing the filter. For borderline cases I would offer an opt-in flag that allows a bounded post-filter over a capped candidate pool and reports that the result may contain fewer than top_k items. The reason is that unbounded scans would let one client degrade latency for everyone, so the contract should make cost visible and bounded."},{"kind":"code","answer":"function solve(input) {\n  const out = [];\n  for (const raw of String(input).split(\"\\n\")) {\n    const line = raw.trim();\n    if (line === \"\") continue;\n    const idx = line.indexOf(\":\");\n    const id = idx === -1 ? line : line.slice(0, idx).trim();\n    const rest = idx === -1 ? \"\" : line.slice(idx + 1);\n    let sum = 0;\n    for (const part of rest.split(\",\")) {\n      const t = part.trim();\n      if (t === \"\") continue;\n      sum += parseInt(t, 10);\n    }\n    out.push(id + \":\" + sum);\n  }\n  return out.join(\"\\n\");\n}"}],"_tests":{"passed":3,"total":3,"results":[{"input":"doc1:3,1,4\ndoc2:10,-2","expected":"doc1:8\ndoc2:8","got":"doc1:8\ndoc2:8","pass":true},{"input":"a:1\n\n  \nb:2,3","expected":"a:1\nb:5","got":"a:1\nb:5","pass":true},{"input":"\n\n","expected":"","got":"","pass":true}]},"grade":{"total":0.917,"parts":[{"kind":"short","score":0.9,"confidence":0.85,"note":"Clearly explains lazy generators versus materialized lists, gives a large bulk-insert case where streaming helps and a small reusable-list case where it does not."},{"kind":"scenario","score":0.9,"confidence":0.8,"note":"Specifies a concrete request shape, uses keyset cursors pinned to a snapshot instead of offset, and rejects costly filters with a clear error plus a bounded opt-in fallback."},{"kind":"code","score":0.95,"confidence":0.9,"note":"Hidden tests pass 3/3; splits lines, skips blanks, parses the first colon, sums integers, preserves order, and returns an empty string when nothing remains.","testsPassed":3,"testsTotal":3}],"regraded":false},"final":0.8154},"c127":{"id":"c127","name":"Pooja Desai","semantic":0.736853128992877,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c178":{"id":"c178","name":"Neha Okafor","semantic":0.736744943441166,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c122":{"id":"c122","name":"Yuki Haddad","semantic":0.779754071897791,"seen":3,"coverage":{"m1":0.645019715101355,"m2":0.6516585494256081,"m3":0.5901246424509697,"n1":0.6795997463657473,"n2":0.6471191038994872},"evidence":[{"requirementId":"m1","start":578,"end":674,"quote":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.97},{"requirementId":"m2","start":262,"end":385,"quote":"Projects: Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.97},{"requirementId":"m3","start":130,"end":261,"quote":"She would describe her strengths as: REST contract design, FastAPI services, PostgreSQL query tuning, pgvector and FAISS retrieval.","strength":"partial","by":"claude","confidence":0.7}],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[],"assessment":{"id":"asm_vzhb93_c122","questions":[{"kind":"short","prompt":"You have a Postgres table of about 50 million rows, each with a 768-dimension embedding stored using the pgvector extension. Approximate nearest-neighbor queries are slow. Which index type would you consider, what do its main build-time and query-time trade-offs look like, and what would you tune first?","rubric":"A strong answer names HNSW and IVFFlat as the main pgvector options and explains that HNSW usually gives better recall and query latency at the cost of slower builds and more memory, while IVFFlat builds faster but needs training data and a sensible list count. It should mention tuning query-time parameters such as ef_search or probes and checking recall against exact search before changing the index.","targets":["Postgres"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"scenario","prompt":"Your team switches to a new embedding model. The existing 40 million stored vectors come from the old model and cannot be compared with new query embeddings. Describe how you would migrate the embeddings pipeline and the search service with no downtime, and how you would confirm the new index is good before cutting over.","rubric":"A good answer covers versioning the embeddings by model, backfilling new vectors into a separate column or table in batches with idempotent jobs, and dual-writing new ingests during the backfill. It should describe a shadow or offline evaluation on a sample of queries comparing relevance, then a gradual traffic switch with a rollback path.","targets":["Embeddings pipeline","Postgres"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"code","prompt":"Write a JavaScript function solve(input) that takes a string and returns a string. The first line of input is a positive integer N, the maximum number of words per chunk. The rest of the input is text. Split the text on any whitespace, group the words into chunks of at most N words in order, and join each chunk's words with a single space. Join the chunks with a newline character. If the text has no words, return an empty string. Do not use any libraries.","rubric":"A correct answer parses N from the first line, splits the remaining text on whitespace while ignoring empty tokens, groups words into chunks of size N with the last chunk possibly shorter, and joins chunks with newlines. It should return an empty string when there are no words. A clean solution takes a few minutes.","targets":["Embeddings pipeline"],"tests":[{"input":"3\na b c d e f g","expected":"a b c\nd e f\ng"},{"input":"2\nhello world","expected":"hello world"},{"input":"2\n","expected":""}]}],"emailSubject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer, Retrieval Platform role","emailBody":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the Senior Backend Engineer role on the Retrieval Platform team. We would like you to complete a short assessment before we move forward.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks. The first is a short written answer about Postgres and vector indexing. The second is a scenario about migrating an embeddings pipeline. The third is a small JavaScript coding task with a function called solve.\n\nPlease allow about 45 minutes. You do not need to finish everything perfectly. We want to see how you reason through the problems.\n\nReply to this email when you are done, or let us know if you have questions about the format.\n\nThe hiring team","unverified":["Postgres","Embeddings pipeline"]},"sent":{"channel":"outbox","at":1793425972611},"responses":[{"kind":"short","answer":"I'd probably go with HNSW since it's usually the fastest for queries. The build is slower and uses more memory, but it gives good recall. I'd start by tuning ef_search and maybe m, and then see how latency looks."},{"kind":"scenario","answer":"I'd run the new model alongside the old one and re-embed the data in batches. Once it's ready, I'd switch the search service over to the new index. Before cutting over I'd check that the results look reasonable on a few queries and make sure nothing breaks."},{"kind":"code","answer":"function solve(input) {\n  const lines = input.split('\\n');\n  const n = parseInt(lines[0]);\n  const words = lines.slice(1).join(' ').split(' ');\n  const chunks = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i += n) {\n    chunks.push(words.slice(i, i + n).join(' '));\n  }\n  return chunks.join('\\n');\n}"}],"_tests":{"passed":3,"total":3,"results":[{"input":"3\na b c d e f g","expected":"a b c\nd e f\ng","got":"a b c\nd e f\ng","pass":true},{"input":"2\nhello world","expected":"hello world","got":"hello world","pass":true},{"input":"2\n","expected":"","got":"","pass":true}]},"grade":{"total":0.417,"parts":[{"kind":"short","score":0.35,"confidence":0.8,"note":"Correctly names HNSW and the build/memory trade-off and mentions ef_search, but omits IVFFlat, its training and list-count needs, probes, and checking recall against exact search."},{"kind":"scenario","score":0.2,"confidence":0.85,"note":"Only vaguely mentions running models side by side and re-embedding in batches; it lacks model versioning, dual-writes, offline or shadow relevance evaluation, gradual traffic shifting, and a rollback path."},{"kind":"code","score":0.7,"confidence":0.6,"note":"Passes all 3 hidden tests and handles chunking and empty input, but splits on a literal single space rather than any whitespace and does not filter empty tokens, so repeated spaces or tabs would yield wrong chunks.","testsPassed":3,"testsTotal":3}],"regraded":false},"final":0.6347},"c002":{"id":"c002","name":"Hana Tanaka","semantic":0.773374595656848,"seen":3,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c130":{"id":"c130","name":"Nora Mukherjee","semantic":0.734325027111168,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c147":{"id":"c147","name":"Ivan Desai","semantic":0.690112200412909,"seen":2,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_003443":{"id":"R_003443","name":"Kenji Saxena","semantic":0.673655410815961,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_006178":{"id":"R_006178","name":"Hana Saxena","semantic":0.672653732542854,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_005850":{"id":"R_005850","name":"Hana Haddad","semantic":0.672086815847032,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_003453":{"id":"R_003453","name":"Aditya Saxena","semantic":0.671609856314747,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_000198":{"id":"R_000198","name":"Aditya Verma","semantic":0.669651466815832,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_001150":{"id":"R_001150","name":"Elena Novak","semantic":0.664743880488175,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_007143":{"id":"R_007143","name":"Priya Verma","semantic":0.726823378626024,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_008727":{"id":"R_008727","name":"Ivan Iyer","semantic":0.713781531202699,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_007420":{"id":"R_007420","name":"Aditya Desai","se