de":"decide","payload":{"task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"no","confidence":0.72,"costUsd":0.00032,"input":{"question":"You have a Postgres table of about 50 million rows, each with a 768-dimension embedding stored using the pgvector extension. Approximate nearest-neighbor que…","rubric":"A strong answer names HNSW and IVFFlat as the main pgvector options and explains that HNSW usually gives better recall and query latency at the cost of slowe…","answer":"I'd probably go with HNSW since it's usually the fastest for queries. The build is slower and uses more memory, but it gives good recall. I'd start by tuning…"},"rationale":"The answer correctly identifies HNSW, its slower build and higher memory cost, and ef_search as a query-time knob. However, it never mentions IVFFlat or its build-time trade-offs (faster build, need for training data, list count). It also does not mention checking recall against exact search before tuning. Suggesting tuning m first is questionable because m is a build-time parameter that requires rebuilding the index. Overall it is partially correct but misses several expected qualities."},"seq":166,"ts":1793426017019},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","kind":"llm","node":"llm","payload":{"purpose":"grade","cached":false,"costUsd":0.0005,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ms":3954,"usage":{"input":1172,"output":762,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0}},"seq":167,"ts":1793426020973},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","kind":"llm","node":"llm","payload":{"purpose":"decide:short_answer_pass","cached":false,"costUsd":0.00018,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ms":1505,"usage":{"input":765,"output":213,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0}},"seq":168,"ts":1793426022478},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","kind":"note","node":"llm","payload":{"text":"Structured output for decision failed validation ([\n  {\n    \"code\": \"invalid_type\",\n    \"expected\": \"number\",\n    \"received\": \"string\",\n    \"path\": [\n      \"confidence\"\n ); retrying once with a repair prompt","purpose":"decide:short_answer_pass"},"seq":169,"ts":1793426022479},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","kind":"llm","node":"llm","payload":{"purpose":"decide:short_answer_pass:repair","cached":false,"costUsd":0.0002,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ms":1074,"usage":{"input":1071,"output":185,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0}},"seq":170,"ts":1793426023553},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","kind":"decision","node":"decide","payload":{"task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.92,"costUsd":0.000383,"input":{"question":"Explain the difference between an idempotent HTTP method and a safe one. Then give one concrete case where an embeddings ingestion endpoint must be idempoten…","rubric":"A strong answer defines safe as no server-side state change and idempotent as repeated identical requests producing the same end state. It names a real inges…","answer":"A safe HTTP method is one that is expected not to change server state. GET, HEAD and OPTIONS are safe. An idempotent method is one where sending the same req…"},"rationale":"Correctly defines safe (no server-side state change) and idempotent (same end state after repeated identical requests), noting every safe method is idempotent but not vice versa. Gives a concrete ingestion case (retry after timeout with lost response) and proposes multiple enforcement mechanisms: stable or content-hash-derived IDs, upsert with a unique constraint on (doc_id, model_version), and Idempotency-Key for POST, plus a replay test. Fully meets the expected qualities."},"seq":171,"ts":1793426023554},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","kind":"llm","node":"llm","payload":{"purpose":"grade","cached":false,"costUsd":0.00057,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ms":3226,"usage":{"input":2333,"output":677,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0}},"seq":172,"ts":1793426026780},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","kind":"llm","node":"llm","payload":{"purpose":"decide:short_answer_pass","cached":false,"costUsd":0.00012,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ms":1255,"usage":{"input":583,"output":130,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrit