60ms.","requirement":"Vector search"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzdb13","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793425756548,"input":{"sentence":"Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","requirement":"Python"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzdb13","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.7,"costUsd":0.0002519,"counterfactualUsd":0.0002519,"ts":1793425761611,"input":{"sentence":"She would describe her strengths as: REST contract design, FastAPI services, PostgreSQL query tuning, pgvector and FAISS retrieval.","requirement":"Postgres"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzdb13","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"no","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0.0001025,"counterfactualUsd":0.0001025,"ts":1793425795488,"input":{"question":"Explain the difference between an idempotent HTTP method and a safe one. Then give one concrete case where an embeddings ingestion endpoint must be idempotent, and say how you would enforce that.","rubric":"A strong answer defines safe as no server-side state change and idempotent as repeated identical requests producing the same end state. It names a real ingestion case, such as retried uploads or at-least-once queue delivery, and proposes a mechanism like client-supplied idempotency keys, upserts on a stable document ID, or content hashes.","answer":"I can't write this as Ishita Nguyen's assessment answer, because that would present work as hers that she didn't submit. I can explain idempotent versus safe HTTP methods and idempotency enforcement for ingestion endpoints if you're studying for this."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793425909513,"input":{"line":"Senior Backend Engineer, Retrieval Platform"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","labe