I gateway with batched inference and warm caches. Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints. Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms. Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.95,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793425923210,"input":{"sentence":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","requirement":"Vector search"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793425924344,"input":{"sentence":"Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","requirement":"Python"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793425925603,"input":{"sentence":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","requirement":"Postgres"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","task":"injection_check","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793425927665,"input":{"text":"Elena Bose. Builder focused on sentence-encoder serving.\nElena studied at Carrow National Institute and has 8 years of experience. She would describe her strengths as: pgvector and FAISS retrieval, sentence-encoder serving, FastAPI services, dense passage ranking, REST contract design.\nProjects: Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set. Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms. Designed REST contracts for a dense passage ranking service used by three internal teams. Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.92,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793425929721,"input":{"sentence":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","requirement":"Python"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.95,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793425931248,"input":{"sentence":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","requirement":"Vector search"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0.0000885,"counterfactualUsd":0.0000885,"ts":1793425933823,"input":{"sentence":"Projects: Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","requirement":"Postgres"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","task":"injection_check","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793425938101,"input":{"text":"Yuki Haddad. Engineer who likes dense passage ranking.\nYuki studied at Pinecrest Community College and has 9 years of experience. She would describe her strengths as: REST contract design, FastAPI services, PostgreSQL query tuning, pgvector and FAISS retrieval.\nProjects: Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints. Designed REST contracts for a dense passage ranking service used by three internal teams. Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches. Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793425940369,"input":{"sentence":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","requirement":"Vector search"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0.000084,"counterfactualUsd":0.000084,"ts":1793425944040,"input":{"sentence":"Projects: Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","requirement":"Python"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.7,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793425945386,"input":{"sentence":"She would describe her strengths as: REST contract design, FastAPI services, PostgreSQL query tuning, pgvector and FAISS retrieval.","requirement":"Postgres"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.92,"costUsd":0.0001381,"counterfactualUsd":0.0001381,"ts":1793426010239,"input":{"question":"Explain the difference between an idempotent HTTP method and a safe one. Then give one concrete case where an embeddings ingestion endpoint must be idempotent, and say how you would enforce that.","rubric":"A strong answer defines safe as no server-side state change and idempotent as repeated identical requests producing the same end state. It names a real ingestion case, such as retried uploads or at-least-once queue delivery, and proposes a mechanism like client-supplied idempotency keys, upserts on a stable document ID, or content hashes.","answer":"A safe method, such as GET or HEAD, is meant to be read-only, so it should not change server state. An idempotent method, such as PUT or DELETE, can be repeated with the same effect as a single call. Every safe method is also idempotent, but not the reverse. A concrete case is an ingestion endpoint like PUT /documents/{doc_id}/embedding. If a worker retries after a timeout, the document should still end up with exactly one current vector and not a duplicate row. To enforce this, I would key the write on doc_id plus a content hash, put a unique constraint on that key in pgvector, and use an upsert. I would also add an idempotency key header for POST-style batch calls so retries return the stored result."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"no","confidence":0.72,"costUsd":0.00032010000000000003,"counterfactualUsd":0.00032010000000000003,"ts":1793426017018,"input":{"question":"You have a Postgres table of about 50 million rows, each with a 768-dimension embedding stored using the pgvector extension. Approximate nearest-neighbor queries are slow. Which index type would you consider, what do its main build-time and query-time trade-offs look like, and what would you tune first?","rubric":"A strong answer names HNSW and IVFFlat as the main pgvector options and explains that HNSW usually gives better recall and query latency at the cost of slower builds and more memory, while IVFFlat builds faster but needs training data and a sensible list count. It should mention tuning query-time parameters such as ef_search or probes and checking recall against exact search before changing the index.","answer":"I'd probably go with HNSW since it's usually the fastest for queries. The build is slower and uses more memory, but it gives good recall. I'd start by tuning ef_search and maybe m, and then see how latency looks."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.92,"costUsd":0.00038260000000000003,"counterfactualUsd":0.00038260000000000003,"ts":1793426023554,"input":{"question":"Explain the difference between an idempotent HTTP method and a safe one. Then give one concrete case where an embeddings ingestion endpoint must be idempotent, and say how you would enforce that.","rubric":"A strong answer defines safe as no server-side state change and idempotent as repeated identical requests producing the same end state. It names a real ingestion case, such as retried uploads or at-least-once queue delivery, and proposes a mechanism like client-supplied idempotency keys, upserts on a stable document ID, or content hashes.","answer":"A safe HTTP method is one that is expected not to change server state. GET, HEAD and OPTIONS are safe. An idempotent method is one where sending the same request N times leaves the server in the same state as sending it once. Every safe method is idempotent, but not the reverse: PUT and DELETE are idempotent without being safe.\n\nA concrete case where an embeddings ingestion endpoint must be idempotent is a retry after a timeout. A batch worker sends PUT /v1/documents/{doc_id}/embedding with a vector and a model_version. The request reaches the server and is written, but the response is lost, so the worker retries. Without idempotency the retry could create duplicate rows or double-count the document in the index.\n\nI would enforce this in three layers. First, derive doc_id from the source system's stable key, or from a content hash if no stable key exists, so the same document always maps to the same identity. Second, make the write an upsert keyed on (doc_id, model_version) with a unique constraint in Postgres, so a duplicate write overwrites rather than inserts. Third, for POST-based batch jobs, require an Idempotency-Key header, store the key with the response for a bounded window, and return the stored response on replay. I would also add a test that replays the same batch twice and asserts the row count and index size are unchanged."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0.00026890000000000003,"counterfactualUsd":0.00026890000000000003,"ts":1793426029031,"input":{"question":"In Python, what is the practical difference between a list comprehension and a generator expression when you pass the result to a function that consumes it once, such as sum() or a database bulk insert? Give one case where the choice matters for memory and one where it does not.","rubric":"A good answer says a generator expression yields items lazily and keeps memory roughly constant, while a list comprehension materializes the whole sequence first. It should give a concrete case where streaming rows into a bulk insert or a large sum() benefits from a generator, and a case where the data is small or must be reused or indexed, so a list is fine.","answer":"A list comprehension builds the whole list in memory before the consumer runs, so peak memory grows with the number of items. A generator expression yields one item at a time and keeps only the current item plus its own small state. Where it matters: loading a few million rows from a file and inserting them with a bulk insert. A list comprehension holds every row at once, while a generator streams them into the driver in batches and keeps memory roughly flat. Where it does not matter: summing a small, already-bounded list such as 50 numeric scores. Both finish in microseconds, the list is cheap, and the list is easier to reuse or debug. I use generators when the data is large or unbounded and the consumer reads it once, and lists when the result is small or needed more than once."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426086936,"input":{"line":"Senior Backend Engineer, Retrieval Platform"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426086971,"input":{"line":"We are building a retrieval platform."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426087019,"input":{"line":"You will own our vector search stack and the embeddings pipeline that feeds it, ship semantic search features, tune Postgres, write Python services, and lead API design for internal consumers."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426087033,"input":{"line":"Must have: vector search, Python, Postgres."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426087051,"input":{"line":"Nice to have: embeddings pipeline, API design."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426087063,"input":{"line":"Seniority: senior."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"yes","confidence":0.95,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426087063,"input":{"line":"Only female candidates under 28 from IIT or Northfield."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"yes","confidence":0.95,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426087063,"input":{"line":"Must be unmarried."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"injection_check","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426092542,"input":{"text":"Ishita Nguyen. Builder focused on pgvector and faiss retrieval.\nIshita studied at Westbrook University and has 11 years of experience. She would describe her strengths as: REST contract design, dense passage ranking, sentence-encoder serving, FastAPI services, pgvector and FAISS retrieval.\nProjects: Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches. Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints. Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms. Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426095730,"input":{"sentence":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","requirement":"Vector search"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426095741,"input":{"sentence":"Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","requirement":"Python"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426095751,"input":{"sentence":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","requirement":"Postgres"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0.0001592,"counterfactualUsd":0.0001592,"ts":1793426097234,"input":{"sentence":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","requirement":"Embeddings pipeline"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.88,"costUsd":0.0001271,"counterfactualUsd":0.0001271,"ts":1793426098535,"input":{"sentence":"Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","requirement":"API design"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"injection_check","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426100041,"input":{"text":"Yuki Haddad. Engineer who likes dense passage ranking.\nYuki studied at Pinecrest Community College and has 9 years of experience. She would describe her strengths as: REST contract design, FastAPI services, PostgreSQL query tuning, pgvector and FAISS retrieval.\nProjects: Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints. Designed REST contracts for a dense passage ranking service used by three internal teams. Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches. Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426102942,"input":{"sentence":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","requirement":"Vector search"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426102959,"input":{"sentence":"Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","requirement":"Python"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.7,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426102975,"input":{"sentence":"She would describe her strengths as: REST contract design, FastAPI services, PostgreSQL query tuning, pgvector and FAISS retrieval.","requirement":"Postgres"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"injection_check","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426106763,"input":{"text":"Aditya Novak. Builder focused on pgvector and faiss retrieval. Captains the men's football team at work.\nAditya studied at Greenhill Institute and has 6 years of experience. He would describe his strengths as: pgvector and FAISS retrieval, REST contract design, FastAPI services, dense passage ranking.\nProjects: Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints. Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set. Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms. Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426108675,"input":{"sentence":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","requirement":"Vector search"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426108686,"input":{"sentence":"Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","requirement":"Python"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426108696,"input":{"sentence":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","requirement":"Postgres"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426108706,"input":{"sentence":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","requirement":"Embeddings pipeline"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"injection_check","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426110549,"input":{"text":"Elena Bose. Builder focused on sentence-encoder serving.\nElena studied at Carrow National Institute and has 8 years of experience. She would describe her strengths as: pgvector and FAISS retrieval, sentence-encoder serving, FastAPI services, dense passage ranking, REST contract design.\nProjects: Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set. Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms. Designed REST contracts for a dense passage ranking service used by three internal teams. Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426112864,"input":{"sentence":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","requirement":"Vector search"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.92,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426112875,"input":{"sentence":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","requirement":"Python"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426112887,"input":{"sentence":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","requirement":"Postgres"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426112898,"input":{"sentence":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","requirement":"Embeddings pipeline"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0.0000694,"counterfactualUsd":0.0000694,"ts":1793426113728,"input":{"sentence":"Designed REST contracts for a dense passage ranking service used by three internal teams.","requirement":"API design"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"injection_check","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426115820,"input":{"text":"Hana Tanaka. Builder focused on dense passage ranking.\nHana studied at Greenhill Institute and has 6 years of experience. She would describe her strengths as: dense passage ranking, PostgreSQL query tuning, FastAPI services.\nProjects: Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms. Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches. Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set. Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426118243,"input":{"sentence":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","requirement":"Vector search"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426118255,"input":{"sentence":"Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","requirement":"Python"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426118267,"input":{"sentence":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","requirement":"Postgres"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426118277,"input":{"sentence":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","requirement":"Embeddings pipeline"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0.0001457,"counterfactualUsd":0.0001457,"ts":1793426176550,"input":{"question":"You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.","rubric":"A good answer proposes a concrete request and response shape, such as a JSON body with query vector or text, a filter object, and a limit with an opaque cursor for pagination rather than offset-based paging on large result sets. It also explains validating filters against an allowlist of fields with clear 4xx errors, and versioning or additive-only changes to keep clients stable.","answer":"I would use a POST or GET search endpoint with a typed request body: `query` (text or vector reference), `limit` (default 20, hard cap 100), `cursor` (opaque string, not a raw offset), and `filters` as an object with an explicit allowlist of fields and operators. The response returns `items` (each with `id`, `score`, and a `metadata` object), plus `next_cursor` (null at the end) and `schema_version`. Cursors encode the last sort key plus a snapshot marker so paging stays stable under concurrent writes. Filters are validated against a versioned schema: unknown fields are rejected with a 400 that names the field, not silently ignored, and new filterable fields are added as optional so older clients keep working. Response fields only grow additively, so clients that ignore unknown keys keep working as the schema evolves."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.6,"costUsd":0.0002631,"counterfactualUsd":0.0002631,"ts":1793426180791,"input":{"question":"A Postgres table stores 50 million document chunks with a 768-dimension embedding column and a tenant_id column. Queries always filter by tenant_id and then need the 10 nearest neighbours by cosine distance. Describe the indexing approach you would use, the trade-offs you would weigh between index types, and one thing you would check in EXPLAIN ANALYZE to confirm the index is used.","rubric":"A strong answer proposes a pgvector HNSW or IVFFlat index on the embedding column, and considers tenant_id through a partial index, partitioning, or a composite approach rather than ignoring the filter. It names a real tradeoff such as build time and memory versus recall and query speed, and names a concrete EXPLAIN ANALYZE check such as confirming an index scan on the vector index with the expected row estimate.","answer":"I'd add an index on the embedding column so the nearest-neighbour search doesn't scan everything. For 50 million rows, I'd probably use pgvector with an HNSW index, since it gives good recall and latency. I'd also think about tenant_id, maybe with a partial index or a btree index on it. The trade-off is that HNSW uses more memory and takes longer to build than IVFFlat, but it's usually faster to query. To check that the index is used, I'd run EXPLAIN ANALYZE and look for an index scan in the plan."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.92,"costUsd":0.00014519999999999998,"counterfactualUsd":0.00014519999999999998,"ts":1793426186416,"input":{"question":"You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.","rubric":"A good answer proposes a concrete request and response shape, such as a JSON body with query vector or text, a filter object, and a limit with an opaque cursor for pagination rather than offset-based paging on large result sets. It also explains validating filters against an allowlist of fields with clear 4xx errors, and versioning or additive-only changes to keep clients stable.","answer":"I'd use a POST or GET search request with a query string, a `limit` (default 20, max 100) and an opaque `cursor` for pagination, plus a `filters` object whose keys are allow-listed and typed. The response would return `items` with `id`, `score`, and `metadata`, plus `next_cursor` and a `schema_version` field. Cursors would encode the last sort key rather than an offset, so inserts don't shift pages. For filter validation I'd validate against a versioned schema at the edge and reject unknown keys with a 400 that names the offending field, so new fields can be added without older clients silently changing results."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.92,"costUsd":0.0001715,"counterfactualUsd":0.0001715,"ts":1793426191550,"input":{"question":"You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.","rubric":"A good answer proposes a concrete request and response shape, such as a JSON body with query vector or text, a filter object, and a limit with an opaque cursor for pagination rather than offset-based paging on large result sets. It also explains validating filters against an allowlist of fields with clear 4xx errors, and versioning or additive-only changes to keep clients stable.","answer":"I would expose a GET /v1/search endpoint that takes a query string or vector reference, a filters object keyed by declared metadata fields, and a cursor plus limit for pagination. The response would return a results array of {id, score, metadata subset} plus a next_cursor that is opaque to clients, so I can change the underlying offset or keyset strategy later without breaking them. For pagination I would use keyset cursors over (score, id) rather than offsets, since offsets drift when the index is updated and get expensive at depth. For filters I would validate against a versioned schema at the gateway: unknown filter keys return a 400 with the list of supported fields, types and ranges are checked, and filters are normalized before they reach the index. To keep the API stable as the schema evolves, I would only add optional fields and filters, never change the meaning of existing ones, and document unknown-field handling as ignore-on-input and omit-on-output for clients that do not request them."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.8,"costUsd":0.0001823,"counterfactualUsd":0.0001823,"ts":1793426196163,"input":{"question":"You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.","rubric":"A good answer proposes a concrete request and response shape, such as a JSON body with query vector or text, a filter object, and a limit with an opaque cursor for pagination rather than offset-based paging on large result sets. It also explains validating filters against an allowlist of fields with clear 4xx errors, and versioning or additive-only changes to keep clients stable.","answer":"I would take a page size and an opaque cursor as query params, and return a list of results with a next_cursor field. Filters would be passed as a small set of named metadata fields, validated with a Pydantic model so unknown fields get rejected. I would keep the response envelope simple so new fields can be added later without breaking clients."}}],"memory":[{"roleKey":"retrieval-backend","runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","note":"Senior Backend Engineer, Retrieval Platform. Must-haves: vector search, Python, Postgres. Nice: embeddings pipeline, API design. The pool's strongest matches were pgvector/FAISS/HNSW + FastAPI profiles, found with a pgvector-focused query. Postgres evidence is often a strengths-list mention, so cite project sentences such as the pgvector/HNSW migration. Five candidates were fully evidenced and approved (c156, c122, c049, c066, c002). Assessment on API design and embeddings gave grades 0.93, 0.93, 0.68, 0.45, 0.28; the code tests were the main differentiator. c122 had only partial Postgres evidence and the lowest grade. The JD contained unlawful criteria (gender, age, college, marital status); these were ignored and not used in any judgement.","preferredSkills":["vector search (pgvector, FAISS, HNSW)","Python services (FastAPI, asyncio)","PostgreSQL tuning","embeddings pipeline","API design (REST contracts, pagination)"],"ts":1793426207016}],"outbox":[{"id":"msg_mvvzemcy_c156","runId":"run_mvvzdb13","candidateId":"c156","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","body":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the Senior Backend Engineer role on the Retrieval Platform team. We are moving you to the next stage.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks. One is a short written question. One is a scenario about planning a migration of an embeddings pipeline. The last is a small JavaScript function with hidden tests.\n\nPlease set aside about 45 minutes. Work on it in one sitting if you can. Answer in plain text or code. Short answers are fine.\n\nReply to this email when you have finished, or tell us if you need more time.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. Explain the difference between an idempotent HTTP method and a safe one. Then give one concrete case where an embeddings ingestion endpoint must be idempotent, and say how you would enforce that.\n\n2. You own the pipeline that embeds about 40 million documents into a vector index. The team wants to switch to a new embedding model with different dimensions. Production search must keep working throughout, and the cutover must be reversible. Describe how you would plan and run the migration. Cover how you store and version vectors, how you backfill, how you validate quality before switching traffic, and how you roll back.\n\n3. Implement solve(input) in JavaScript. The input is a JSON string with fields \"text\" (string), \"size\" (positive integer), and \"overlap\" (integer, 0 <= overlap < size). Split text into chunks of up to size characters. Each new chunk starts size minus overlap characters after the previous start. Stop after the first chunk that reaches the end of the text. Return a JSON string of the array of chunks. Do not add a trailing chunk that is wholly contained in the previous one. Example: text \"abcdefghij\", size 4, overlap 1 gives [\"abcd\",\"defg\",\"ghij\"].","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793425782706},{"id":"msg_mvvziow3_c049","runId":"run_mvvzhb93","candidateId":"c049","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","body":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the Senior Backend Engineer role on the Retrieval Platform team. We are moving you to the next stage.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks. One is a short written question. One is a scenario about planning a migration of an embeddings pipeline. The last is a small JavaScript function with hidden tests.\n\nPlease set aside about 45 minutes. Work on it in one sitting if you can. Answer in plain text or code. Short answers are fine.\n\nReply to this email when you have finished, or tell us if you need more time.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. Explain the difference between an idempotent HTTP method and a safe one. Then give one concrete case where an embeddings ingestion endpoint must be idempotent, and say how you would enforce that.\n\n2. You own the pipeline that embeds about 40 million documents into a vector index. The team wants to switch to a new embedding model with different dimensions. Production search must keep working throughout, and the cutover must be reversible. Describe how you would plan and run the migration. Cover how you store and version vectors, how you backfill, how you validate quality before switching traffic, and how you roll back.\n\n3. Implement solve(input) in JavaScript. The input is a JSON string with fields \"text\" (string), \"size\" (positive integer), and \"overlap\" (integer, 0 <= overlap < size). Split text into chunks of up to size characters. Each new chunk starts size minus overlap characters after the previous start. Stop after the first chunk that reaches the end of the text. Return a JSON string of the array of chunks. Do not add a trailing chunk that is wholly contained in the previous one. Example: text \"abcdefghij\", size 4, overlap 1 gives [\"abcd\",\"defg\",\"ghij\"].","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793425972611},{"id":"msg_mvvziow3_c122","runId":"run_mvvzhb93","candidateId":"c122","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer, Retrieval Platform role","body":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the Senior Backend Engineer role on the Retrieval Platform team. We would like you to complete a short assessment before we move forward.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks. The first is a short written answer about Postgres and vector indexing. The second is a scenario about migrating an embeddings pipeline. The third is a small JavaScript coding task with a function called solve.\n\nPlease allow about 45 minutes. You do not need to finish everything perfectly. We want to see how you reason through the problems.\n\nReply to this email when you are done, or let us know if you have questions about the format.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. You have a Postgres table of about 50 million rows, each with a 768-dimension embedding stored using the pgvector extension. Approximate nearest-neighbor queries are slow. Which index type would you consider, what do its main build-time and query-time trade-offs look like, and what would you tune first?\n\n2. Your team switches to a new embedding model. The existing 40 million stored vectors come from the old model and cannot be compared with new query embeddings. Describe how you would migrate the embeddings pipeline and the search service with no downtime, and how you would confirm the new index is good before cutting over.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function solve(input) that takes a string and returns a string. The first line of input is a positive integer N, the maximum number of words per chunk. The rest of the input is text. Split the text on any whitespace, group the words into chunks of at most N words in order, and join each chunk's words with a single space. Join the chunks with a newline character. If the text has no words, return an empty string. Do not use any libraries.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793425972611},{"id":"msg_mvvziow3_c156","runId":"run_mvvzhb93","candidateId":"c156","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","body":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the Senior Backend Engineer role on the Retrieval Platform team. We are moving you to the next stage.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks. One is a short written question. One is a scenario about planning a migration of an embeddings pipeline. The last is a small JavaScript function with hidden tests.\n\nPlease set aside about 45 minutes. Work on it in one sitting if you can. Answer in plain text or code. Short answers are fine.\n\nReply to this email when you have finished, or tell us if you need more time.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. Explain the difference between an idempotent HTTP method and a safe one. Then give one concrete case where an embeddings ingestion endpoint must be idempotent, and say how you would enforce that.\n\n2. You own the pipeline that embeds about 40 million documents into a vector index. The team wants to switch to a new embedding model with different dimensions. Production search must keep working throughout, and the cutover must be reversible. Describe how you would plan and run the migration. Cover how you store and version vectors, how you backfill, how you validate quality before switching traffic, and how you roll back.\n\n3. Implement solve(input) in JavaScript. The input is a JSON string with fields \"text\" (string), \"size\" (positive integer), and \"overlap\" (integer, 0 <= overlap < size). Split text into chunks of up to size characters. Each new chunk starts size minus overlap characters after the previous start. Stop after the first chunk that reaches the end of the text. Return a JSON string of the array of chunks. Do not add a trailing chunk that is wholly contained in the previous one. Example: text \"abcdefghij\", size 4, overlap 1 gives [\"abcd\",\"defg\",\"ghij\"].","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793425972611},{"id":"msg_mvvziow3_c066","runId":"run_mvvzhb93","candidateId":"c066","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Senior Backend Engineer, Retrieval Platform: next step","body":"Hi,\n\nThank you for applying for the Senior Backend Engineer role on the Retrieval Platform team. We would like to move to the next step.\n\nThe role covers vector search, Postgres, the embeddings pipeline, and the services that sit on top of them. We want to see how you think about two areas in particular: Python and API design.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks. One is a short written question. One is a scenario about designing an endpoint. The last is a small coding task in JavaScript that takes about 15 minutes on its own. Plan on about 45 minutes in total. Answer in plain prose or code. There is no need to polish the wording.\n\nPlease reply within three days with your answers. If you need more time, tell us and we will work with you.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. In Python, what is the practical difference between a list comprehension and a generator expression when you pass the result to a function that consumes it once, such as sum() or a database bulk insert? Give one case where the choice matters for memory and one where it does not.\n\n2. You are designing a REST endpoint for the retrieval platform that lets clients run a vector similarity search over a collection, with optional metadata filters and pagination. Describe the request and response shape you would use, how you would paginate results when scores can change as new embeddings are added, and how you would handle a client that sends a filter your index cannot serve efficiently. Keep it to the decisions and the reasons for them.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string containing one or more lines. Each line is a chunk id, a colon, and a comma-separated list of integer embedding dimensions, for example \"doc1:3,1,4\". Return a string with one line per input line in the form \"id:N\" where N is the sum of that line's dimensions. Keep the order of the input lines. Ignore blank lines. Return an empty string if there are no non-blank lines.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793425972611},{"id":"msg_mvvzmcx1_c156","runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","candidateId":"c156","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","body":"Hi,\n\nWe are hiring a Senior Backend Engineer for the Retrieval Platform team. The work covers vector search, embeddings pipelines, Postgres, and API design for internal and external clients.\n\nWe are writing because your profile did not show enough detail on API design, so we would like to see how you reason about it. The assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small JavaScript coding task. Please allow about 45 minutes in total.\n\nReply to this email when you are finished. If you have questions about the format, ask before you start.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.\n\n2. Your team owns a public API that returns similarity search results. Two downstream clients have started depending on a field name that you now want to rename, and a third client needs a new ranking score that changes the meaning of an existing field. Describe how you would roll out both changes without breaking current clients, including how you would communicate the timeline and how you would know when it is safe to remove the old behavior.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string containing a JSON array of query objects, each with fields \"id\" (string) and \"tags\" (array of strings). Return a string containing a JSON array of the ids whose tags include both \"public\" and \"indexed\", in the same order as the input. Return the result as JSON, for example [\"a\",\"c\"]. Return \"[]\" if none match.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793426143717},{"id":"msg_mvvzmcx1_c122","runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","candidateId":"c122","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","body":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the Senior Backend Engineer role on the Retrieval Platform team. We are moving you to the next step, which is a short take-home assessment.\n\nThe role covers vector search, Python services, Postgres, the embeddings pipeline, and API design. The assessment checks three areas we could not confirm from your profile: Postgres, the embeddings pipeline, and API design.\n\nThere are three tasks. One is a short written question about Postgres indexing. One is a scenario about rolling out a model change in an embeddings pipeline. The last is a small JavaScript coding task with three test cases.\n\nPlease allow about 45 minutes. Answer in plain text. You can reply to this email with your answers.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. A Postgres table stores 50 million document chunks with a 768-dimension embedding column and a tenant_id column. Queries always filter by tenant_id and then need the 10 nearest neighbours by cosine distance. Describe the indexing approach you would use, the trade-offs you would weigh between index types, and one thing you would check in EXPLAIN ANALYZE to confirm the index is used.\n\n2. Your embeddings pipeline re-embeds a corpus of 20 million documents whenever the embedding model changes. Last month a model upgrade ran for two days, half the vectors ended up in the old index and half in the new one, and search quality dropped for some tenants. Walk through how you would redesign the pipeline so that a model change can be rolled out without mixing vector spaces, how you would verify each stage, and how you would roll back if the new model underperforms.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string and returns a string. The first line of input contains two integers k and s, separated by a space, where k is the window size in words and s is the overlap in words, with 0 <= s < k. The second line contains the words separated by single spaces. Split the words into windows of k words, where each new window starts k - s words after the previous one. Stop after the first window that reaches the last word. Return the windows as lines, each window's words joined by single spaces, with windows separated by \\n. Do not use any external libraries. This should take under 15 minutes.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793426143717},{"id":"msg_mvvzmcx1_c049","runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","candidateId":"c049","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","body":"Hi,\n\nWe are hiring a Senior Backend Engineer for the Retrieval Platform team. The work covers vector search, embeddings pipelines, Postgres, and API design for internal and external clients.\n\nWe are writing because your profile did not show enough detail on API design, so we would like to see how you reason about it. The assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small JavaScript coding task. Please allow about 45 minutes in total.\n\nReply to this email when you are finished. If you have questions about the format, ask before you start.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.\n\n2. Your team owns a public API that returns similarity search results. Two downstream clients have started depending on a field name that you now want to rename, and a third client needs a new ranking score that changes the meaning of an existing field. Describe how you would roll out both changes without breaking current clients, including how you would communicate the timeline and how you would know when it is safe to remove the old behavior.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string containing a JSON array of query objects, each with fields \"id\" (string) and \"tags\" (array of strings). Return a string containing a JSON array of the ids whose tags include both \"public\" and \"indexed\", in the same order as the input. Return the result as JSON, for example [\"a\",\"c\"]. Return \"[]\" if none match.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793426143717},{"id":"msg_mvvzmcx1_c066","runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","candidateId":"c066","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","body":"Hi,\n\nWe are hiring a Senior Backend Engineer for the Retrieval Platform team. The work covers vector search, embeddings pipelines, Postgres, and API design for internal and external clients.\n\nWe are writing because your profile did not show enough detail on API design, so we would like to see how you reason about it. The assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small JavaScript coding task. Please allow about 45 minutes in total.\n\nReply to this email when you are finished. If you have questions about the format, ask before you start.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.\n\n2. Your team owns a public API that returns similarity search results. Two downstream clients have started depending on a field name that you now want to rename, and a third client needs a new ranking score that changes the meaning of an existing field. Describe how you would roll out both changes without breaking current clients, including how you would communicate the timeline and how you would know when it is safe to remove the old behavior.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string containing a JSON array of query objects, each with fields \"id\" (string) and \"tags\" (array of strings). Return a string containing a JSON array of the ids whose tags include both \"public\" and \"indexed\", in the same order as the input. Return the result as JSON, for example [\"a\",\"c\"]. Return \"[]\" if none match.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793426143717},{"id":"msg_mvvzmcx1_c002","runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","candidateId":"c002","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","body":"Hi,\n\nWe are hiring a Senior Backend Engineer for the Retrieval Platform team. The work covers vector search, embeddings pipelines, Postgres, and API design for internal and external clients.\n\nWe are writing because your profile did not show enough detail on API design, so we would like to see how you reason about it. The assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small JavaScript coding task. Please allow about 45 minutes in total.\n\nReply to this email when you are finished. If you have questions about the format, ask before you start.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.\n\n2. Your team owns a public API that returns similarity search results. Two downstream clients have started depending on a field name that you now want to rename, and a third client needs a new ranking score that changes the meaning of an existing field. Describe how you would roll out both changes without breaking current clients, including how you would communicate the timeline and how you would know when it is safe to remove the old behavior.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string containing a JSON array of query objects, each with fields \"id\" (string) and \"tags\" (array of strings). Return a string containing a JSON array of the ids whose tags include both \"public\" and \"indexed\", in the same order as the input. Return the result as JSON, for example [\"a\",\"c\"]. Return \"[]\" if none match.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793426143717}],"approvals":{"run_mvvyg9ub":{"candidateIds":["c178","c122"],"removed":[],"approvedAt":1793424189566},"run_mvvyhdgj":{"candidateIds":["c178","c122"],"removed":[],"approvedAt":1793424236180},"run_mvvysntsbki":{"candidateIds":["c178","c122"],"removed":[],"approvedAt":1793424829047},"run_mvvzdb13":{"candidateIds":["c156","c049","c066","c122"],"removed":[],"approvedAt":1793425768240},"run_mvvzhb93":{"candidateIds":["c049","c122","c156","c066"],"removed":[],"approvedAt":1793425952894},"run_mvvzl4ex":{"candidateIds":["c156","c122","c049","c066","c002"],"removed":[],"approvedAt":1793426121136}}}